{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "module": "commonjs",
    "target": "ES2020",
    "lib": ["ES2020"],
    "outDir": "out",
    "rootDir": "src",
    "sourceMap": true,
    "strict": true,
    // `arr[i]` and `rec[k]` yield `T | undefined`. The point is the reads where
    // nothing proves the element is there — see the owner/name split in
    // adapters/github/marketplace.ts, which this flag caught building a GitHub
    // URL out of `undefined`. Where an invariant does hold, the fix is to make
    // the code carry it (a tuple return, `entries()` over a loop index, a guard
    // that binds what it checks) rather than to assert past the flag.
    "noUncheckedIndexedAccess": true,
    // Zero hits today — nothing in src/ or the suite overrides an inherited
    // member — so this buys no cleanup, only the ratchet. The subclasses that
    // exist are the ones where a silent override would be worth catching:
    // `InjectError`/`BridgeRpcError` over `Error`, whose `name`/`toString` feed
    // the messages users see, and the suite's `FakeChild` over `EventEmitter`,
    // where quietly shadowing `emit` or `on` would make the double lie about
    // the process it stands in for.
    "noImplicitOverride": true,
    // Also zero hits: every case in the message-dispatch switches that each
    // presenter is built around already ends in `return` or `break`. The flag
    // holds that shape. Those switches are the widest branch points in the
    // codebase — a case falling into the next one would run a second webview
    // command nobody asked for — and they grow a case every time a panel gains
    // a message, which is when the mistake would be made.
    "noFallthroughCasesInSwitch": true,
    // `verbatimModuleSyntax` is deliberately absent, and this note is here so
    // the next person does not spend the afternoon finding out why.
    //
    // It is usually described as an import-type discipline flag, and that half
    // of it is already enforced: biome's `style/useImportType` is on via the
    // recommended preset and errors on a type-only import written as a value
    // import — verified by writing one and watching `npm run lint` reject it.
    // Turning the flag on would add nothing there.
    //
    // The other half is not optional and does not fit. Under `"module":
    // "commonjs"` the flag forbids ESM import/export syntax outright, on the
    // grounds that it will not be down-levelled: 767 errors across src/, every
    // one of them TS1287/TS1295 saying this file must become an ES module.
    // Satisfying it means emitting ESM, which is a build change, not a
    // strictness change — the extension host loads `out/extension.js` by
    // `require`, and `vscode` itself is only available as a CommonJS module.
    // The flag belongs to a bundler-and-ESM setup this extension is not.
    "esModuleInterop": true,
    // TypeScript 6 changed the default of `types` from "every @types package
    // under node_modules" to `[]`, so the ambient packages now have to be named
    // or they are simply not loaded — the failure is 200-odd TS2591/TS2304
    // errors claiming `fs`, `process`, `Buffer`, `NodeJS` and `fetch` do not
    // exist. Both entries here are load-bearing and neither is a `@types`
    // package we merely happen to have installed:
    //   node   — every adapter under src/adapters/node is written against it,
    //            and it is also what supplies the `fetch`/`Response` globals
    //            the marketplace adapter uses (via undici-types, not the DOM
    //            lib, which this unit deliberately does not include).
    //   vscode — `import * as vscode from "vscode"` resolves through an ambient
    //            `declare module` rather than a node_modules entry point, so it
    //            disappears entirely if it is not listed.
    // Naming them is the better state regardless of the default: the extension
    // host's globals are now a decision in this file instead of a side effect
    // of the dependency tree.
    "types": ["node", "vscode"],
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "forceConsistentCasingInFileNames": true
  },
  // No `exclude`: `include` already narrows this unit to src/, and the default
  // exclude covers node_modules and outDir. An explicit list here was dead and,
  // being inherited through `extends`, silently subtracted test/ from
  // tsconfig.test.json.
  "include": ["src"]
}
